# How many judgements each test reached, and which tests say nothing.
#
# Every test ends on a line of its own -- "21 checks in 0.05 s". Until
# this file existed nobody read it: a test whose checking part dies
# without throwing prints "0 checks in 2.74 s", then "All good.", and
# leaves green. run.sh holds the number here, per test.
#
# Two kinds of row, tab-separated, the same shape as state/counterproof:
#
#   <test>   <judgements>   the floor. Fewer than this and the test is
#                           red, however green every judgement in it
#                           was. It rises by itself: a green test that
#                           judged more writes the new number down at
#                           the end of the run. It comes down only
#                           through somebody's edit, where a diff shows
#                           it -- a count that fell is red, and a red
#                           test's number is never written back.
#
#   silent   <test>         prints no count at all, so nothing here can
#                           hold it. These are the census of what this
#                           net does not cover, and the census may
#                           shrink and never grow: a test that neither
#                           counts nor stands here is red, so a new one
#                           cannot join them quietly. A test that starts
#                           printing a count moves out of the census by
#                           itself, on its first green run.
#
# A test that says SKIPPED: or LEFT OUT is not held to its floor for
# that run: it has named the piece it could not do, and fewer
# judgements follow from that. Nought judgements is red even then.
#
# Written by run.sh, only from a run against the file this repository
# ships -- a run under VPM_SCRIPT measures and writes nothing.
